In .NET MAUI Shell, there's no built-in "intercept back navigation" hook, so you combine two techniques:

### 1. Override `OnBackButtonPressed` (hardware/software back)

```csharp
// EditProductPage.xaml.cs
protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
{
    if (_viewModel.HasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        Dispatcher.Dispatch(async () =>
        {
            bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
                "Unsaved Changes",
                "Discard changes?",
                "Discard", "Stay");

            if (leave)
                await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
        });

        return true; // cancel the default navigation
    }

    return base.OnBackButtonPressed(); // allow normal back
}
```

> `return true` swallows the back event. The async prompt runs on the dispatcher because `OnBackButtonPressed` is synchronous.

### 2. Replace the Shell nav-bar back button with a "Cancel" button

Hide the default back button and add your own in the toolbar:

```xml
<!-- EditProductPage.xaml -->
<Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
    <BackButtonBehavior IsVisible="False" />
</Shell.BackButtonBehavior>

<ContentPage.ToolbarItems>
    <ToolbarItem Text="Cancel" Command="{Binding CancelCommand}" />
</ContentPage.ToolbarItems>
```

### 3. ViewModel command

```csharp
[RelayCommand]
async Task Cancel()
{
    if (HasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        bool leave = await Shell.Current.DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes", "Discard changes?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (!leave) return;
    }

    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

### Key points

- **`OnBackButtonPressed`** handles Android hardware back and Shell's software back button.
- **`BackButtonBehavior IsVisible="False"`** hides the default "←" so your "Cancel" `ToolbarItem` is the only way back in the nav bar.
- On **iOS swipe-back gesture**, `OnBackButtonPressed` isn't called. To block it, also set `IsEnabled="False"` on `BackButtonBehavior` (this disables the swipe gesture on iOS).
